Benedict Hyman "Dick" Gross [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-Celebratio-becoming-1]  (June 22, 1950 – December 19, 2025) was an American mathematician who was a professor at the  [LINK: (University of California, San Diego) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_California,_San_Diego] , [LINK: ([2]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-2]  the George Vasmer Leverett Professor of Mathematics Emeritus at  [LINK: (Harvard University) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harvard_University] , and  [LINK: (Dean) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dean_(education)]  of  [LINK: (Harvard College) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harvard_College] . [LINK: ([3]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-cv-3]

He is known for his work in  [LINK: (number theory) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Number_theory] , particularly the  [LINK: (Gross–Zagier theorem) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gross%E2%80%93Zagier_theorem]  on  [LINK: (L-functions) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/L-function]  of  [LINK: (elliptic curves) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elliptic_curve] , and related topics in  [LINK: (algebraic geometry) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Algebraic_geometry] ,  [LINK: (automorphic forms) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Automorphic_form] , and  [LINK: (representation theory) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Representation_theory] . He received a  [LINK: (MacArthur Fellowship) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MacArthur_Fellows_Program]  in 1986 and a  [LINK: (Cole Prize) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cole_Prize]  in 1987. He was a fellow of the  [LINK: (American Academy of Arts and Sciences) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_Academy_of_Arts_and_Sciences] , a fellow of the  [LINK: (American Philosophical Society) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_Philosophical_Society] , and an elected member of the  [LINK: (National Academy of Sciences) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Academy_of_Sciences] .


# BIOGRAPHY #

Benedict Hyman Gross was born on June 22, 1950, in  [LINK: (South Orange, New Jersey) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Orange,_New_Jersey] , to Joel Gross and Terry Gross (née Stavisky). [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-Celebratio-becoming-1]  His father, Joel Gross, was born on a farm in  [LINK: (Jersey City) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jersey_City]  to Austrian immigrants who had moved to the United States as teenagers. [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-Celebratio-becoming-1]  His mother, Terry Gross, was born in New York City to Polish immigrants who had also moved to the United States as teenagers. [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-Celebratio-becoming-1]

Gross and his family moved to  [LINK: (Santa Monica, California) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Santa_Monica,_California] , at the age of 5, before returning to New Jersey during second grade. [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-Celebratio-becoming-1]  He attended  [LINK: (West Orange High School) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/West_Orange_High_School_(New_Jersey)] , but transferred out after his freshman year. Gross graduated from The  [LINK: (Pingry School) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pingry_School] , a leading independent school in New Jersey, in 1967 as the valedictorian. [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-Celebratio-becoming-1]

Gross initially studied physics at  [LINK: (Harvard University) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harvard_University] , deterred from mathematics because he thought that his classmates were better prepared than he was, but switched to mathematics in his sophomore year after enjoying the  [LINK: (Math 55) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Math_55]  course that was taught by  [LINK: (Andrew M. Gleason) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andrew_M._Gleason] . [LINK: ([4]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-Harvard-Gazette-Obit-4]  In 1971, he graduated  [LINK: (Phi Beta Kappa) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phi_Beta_Kappa]  from Harvard University. [LINK: ([4]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-Harvard-Gazette-Obit-4]

Gross studied music in Africa and Asia, and then moved to  [LINK: (Oxford University) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oxford_University]  as a  [LINK: (Marshall Scholar) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marshall_Scholar]  where he studied history, sociology, and mathematics. [LINK: ([4]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-Harvard-Gazette-Obit-4]  He received a M.Sc. from Oxford University in 1974, and then returned to Harvard, where he received his Ph.D. in Mathematics in 1978 under the supervision of  [LINK: (John Tate) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Tate_(mathematician)] . [LINK: ([3]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-cv-3]  [LINK: ([5]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-mg-5]

Gross was an assistant professor at  [LINK: (Princeton University) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Princeton_University]  from 1978 to 1982, a  [LINK: (maître de conférences) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ma%C3%AEtre_de_conf%C3%A9rences]  at  [LINK: (Université de Paris VII) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paris_Diderot_University]  in 1980, and an associate professor at  [LINK: (Brown University) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brown_University]  from 1982 to 1985. [LINK: ([3]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-cv-3]  Gross became a tenured professor at Harvard University in 1985 and became the George Vasmer Leverett Professor at Harvard University in 1988. [LINK: ([3]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-cv-3]  He served as Dean of Undergraduate Education from 2002 to 2003 and as the Dean of  [LINK: (Harvard College) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harvard_College]  from 2003 to 2007. [LINK: ([6]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-6]  He retired from Harvard University as a professor emeritus and joined the  [LINK: (University of California, San Diego) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_California,_San_Diego] , as a professor in 2016. [LINK: ([7]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-Harvard-Obit-7]  [LINK: ([8]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-Scripps-8]

Gross was the mathematical consultant for the 1980 film  [LINK: (It's My Turn) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/It%27s_My_Turn_(film)]  containing the scene [LINK: ([9]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-9]  in which actress  [LINK: (Jill Clayburgh) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jill_Clayburgh] , portraying a mathematics professor, impeccably proves the  [LINK: (snake lemma) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snake_lemma] . [LINK: ([10]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-10]  [LINK: ([11]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-Gross_Lights_Camera-11]  Gross was a member of the Board of Trustees of the  [LINK: (Institute for Advanced Study) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Institute_for_Advanced_Study]  from 2012 to 2017 [LINK: ([12]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-12]  [LINK: ([13]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-13]  and the executive committee of the  [LINK: (International Mathematical Union) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Mathematical_Union]  from 2015 to 2019. [LINK: ([14]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-CV-celebratio-14]  He joined the Board of Directors of  [LINK: (Scripps Research) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scripps_Research]  in 2018. [LINK: ([8]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-Scripps-8]


# RESEARCH #

Gross's work spanned topics in number theory,  [LINK: (algebraic geometry) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Algebraic_geometry] ,  [LINK: (modular forms) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Modular_form] , and  [LINK: (group representations) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Group_representation] . [LINK: ([7]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-Harvard-Obit-7]  [LINK: ([15]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-MacArthur-15]  His work with  [LINK: (Don Zagier) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Don_Zagier]  on the  [LINK: (Gross–Zagier formula) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gross%E2%80%93Zagier_formula]  in 1986 has been influential in modern  [LINK: (number theory) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Number_theory] . [LINK: ([16]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-Harvard-Crimson-Obit-16]  It describes the  [LINK: (height) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/N%C3%A9ron%E2%80%93Tate_height]  of Heegner points in terms of a derivative of the  [LINK: (L-function) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/L-function]  of elliptic curves [LINK: ([17]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-17]  and led to breakthroughs on the  [LINK: (class number problem) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Class_number_problem]  of  [LINK: (Carl Friedrich Gauss) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carl_Friedrich_Gauss] . [LINK: ([16]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-Harvard-Crimson-Obit-16]  [LINK: ([15]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-MacArthur-15]


# AWARDS AND HONORS #

Gross received a  [LINK: (MacArthur Fellowship) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MacArthur_Fellows_Program]  in 1986. [LINK: ([15]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-MacArthur-15]  [LINK: ([7]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-Harvard-Obit-7]  Gross, Zagier, and  [LINK: (Dorian M. Goldfeld) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dorian_M._Goldfeld]  won the  [LINK: (Cole Prize) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cole_Prize]  of the  [LINK: (American Mathematical Society) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_Mathematical_Society]  in 1987 for their work on the class number problem and the  [LINK: (Gross–Zagier formula) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gross%E2%80%93Zagier_formula] . [LINK: ([18]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-18]  [LINK: ([19]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-19]  In 2012, he became a fellow of the  [LINK: (American Mathematical Society) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_Mathematical_Society] . [LINK: ([20]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-20]

Gross was elected as a fellow of the  [LINK: (American Academy of Arts and Sciences) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_Academy_of_Arts_and_Sciences]  in 1992 [LINK: ([21]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-21]  and as a member of the  [LINK: (National Academy of Sciences) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Academy_of_Sciences]  in 2004. [LINK: ([22]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-22]  He was elected to the  [LINK: (American Philosophical Society) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_Philosophical_Society]  in 2017. [LINK: ([23]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-23]


# PERSONAL LIFE #

Gross first met his wife  [LINK: (Jill P. Mesirov) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jill_P._Mesirov]  at a party hosted by  [LINK: (Robert Langlands) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Langlands] . [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-Celebratio-becoming-1]  They married in 1982 and had two sons. [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-Celebratio-becoming-1]

Gross was diagnosed with  [LINK: (testicular cancer) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Testicular_cancer]  when he was an undergraduate at Harvard University, a likely effect of his mother having been prescribed  [LINK: (diethylstilbestrol) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diethylstilbestrol]  during pregnancy to prevent  [LINK: (miscarriage) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miscarriage] . [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-Celebratio-becoming-1]  He received radiation therapy to prevent recurrence and had several major abdominal surgeries to deal with issues caused by this radiation over his lifetime. [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-Celebratio-becoming-1]  He died on December 19, 2025, after a long illness. [LINK: ([24]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-24]  [LINK: ([7]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-Harvard-Obit-7]  [LINK: ([16]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-Harvard-Crimson-Obit-16]  [LINK: ([4]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Gross#cite_note-Harvard-Gazette-Obit-4]


# MAJOR PUBLICATIONS #

Gross, Benedict H.; Harris, Joe (1981). "Real algebraic curves". Annales scientifiques de l'École normale supérieure. 14 (2): 157–182.  [LINK: (doi) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doi_(identifier)] :10.24033/asens.1401.  [LINK: (ISSN) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISSN_(identifier)]  0012-9593.

Gross, Benedict H.; Zagier, Don B. (1986). "Heegner points and derivatives of L-series". Inventiones Mathematicae. 84 (2): 225–320.  [LINK: (Bibcode) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bibcode_(identifier)] :1986InMat..84..225G.  [LINK: (doi) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doi_(identifier)] :10.1007/BF01388809.  [LINK: (ISSN) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISSN_(identifier)]  0020-9910.

Gross, B.; Kohnen, W.; Zagier, D. (1987). "Heegner points and derivatives of L-series. II". Mathematische Annalen. 278 (1–4): 497–562.  [LINK: (doi) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doi_(identifier)] :10.1007/BF01458081.  [LINK: (ISSN) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISSN_(identifier)]  0025-5831.

Gross, Benedict H. (1987). "Heights and the special values of L-series". In Kisilevsky, H.; Labute, J. (eds.). Number Theory: Proceedings of the 1985 Montreal Conference Held June 17–29, 1985. Providence, R.I: Published by the American Mathematical Society for the Canadian Mathematical Society. pp. 115–187.  [LINK: (ISBN) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISBN_(identifier)]   [LINK: (978-0-8218-6012-0) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:BookSources/978-0-8218-6012-0] .

Gross, Benedict H. (October 1, 1990). "A tameness criterion for Galois representations associated to modular forms (mod p)". Duke Mathematical Journal. 61 (2): 445–517.  [LINK: (doi) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doi_(identifier)] :10.1215/S0012-7094-90-06119-8.  [LINK: (ISSN) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISSN_(identifier)]  0012-7094.

Gross, Benedict H.; Prasad, Dipendra (October 1, 1992). "On the Decomposition of a Representation of SO n When Restricted to SO n-1". Canadian Journal of Mathematics. 44 (5): 974–1002.  [LINK: (doi) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doi_(identifier)] :10.4153/CJM-1992-060-8.  [LINK: (ISSN) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISSN_(identifier)]  0008-414X.

Gan, Wee Teck; Gross, Benedict H.; Prasad, Dipendra (2012). "Symplectic local root numbers, central critical L-values, and restriction problems in the representation theory of classical groups". Sur les conjectures de Gross et Prasad. Paris: Societé mathématique de France. pp. 1–109.  [LINK: (ISBN) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISBN_(identifier)]   [LINK: (978-2-85629-348-5) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:BookSources/978-2-85629-348-5] .  [LINK: (OCLC) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OCLC_(identifier)]  827954844.


